{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I just started a new job and I feel completely overwhelmed. Is it normal to feel this way?", "response": "Absolutely normal — starting any new role is a lot to take in. You are expected to learn, not to already know everything. Give yourself grace for the first few weeks; most colleagues would rather you ask a \"simple\" question than struggle silently. You have a fresh start here, and every expert was once a beginner."}
{"prompt": "My partner forgot our anniversary and I feel like they don care about me anymore.", "response": "Ouch, that hurts. While forgetting an anniversary stings, one missed date doesn not erase years of care and commitment. People have off days and slip-ups happen. When you are ready, share how it made you feel without assuming the worst about their intentions. Sometimes hearing the impact is all someone needs to mend the gap."}
